294:## 5. Step 2: find and compare related evidence
298:An open thesis may accumulate many evidence records. Step 1 needs to understand the article and the open research; it does not need to read every prior statement to do that. Step 2 uses each proposed `{thesis, evidence}` association to find the history worth comparing.
322:  the same story; a wording change failed its line (Step 2 log §14).
336:**Repeat handoff:** when Jev identifies a repeat, the comparison must retain the identity of the existing evidence record it was compared against. Step 3 uses that identity to place the incoming record in the existing record's group, adding its source and any missing thesis link. The typed Jev response need not generate the record ID: the caller can associate the choice with the candidate it supplied. The rule for several matching candidates is stated above.
338:**Step 2 acceptance (Alex, 2026-11-08).** Three lines apply:
343:The first measurement against these lines is in the [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §12; the isolated modules are `step2/compare.py` and `step3/library.py`.
345:**Frozen (D427, Alex, 2026-11-11: "好jev 收尾冻结").** Step 2 and Step 3's grouping library stay as accepted and measured (Step 2 log §§12–18); `tests/test_frozen_clean_step2.py` fails the suite when one of their files moves. They go to production only with the whole clean path, after Step 4 meets its lines, through an end-to-end comparison against production on the frozen ruler (three draws each), with these three lines checked on that run. A change is a new version that only Alex opens; while he is not active, the council may open it (D439), never the frozen ruler. Five open defects are carried with the freeze (Step 2 log §17).
347:The diagram's intended sequence is: use `{thesis, incoming evidence}` to search the evidence library; pass the returned candidates and incoming evidence to Jev; pass the comparison result and the proposed association to Step 3. For a new thesis, search the same entity-wide evidence library. The frozen Jev request appears in [section 13](#13-step-2-prompt-jev-request-frozen).
349:## 6. Step 3: save evidence and links
351:Step 3 takes the proposals from Step 1 and the comparison outcomes from Step 2 and writes the resulting research information.
355:| New thesis with supporting evidence | The new thesis and its evidence link; save new evidence or reuse a matching record according to Step 2. |
359:| Revision | The new evidence and its source, linked to the earlier statement as a revision. In the judge's ledger the new record also takes the earlier record's thesis links (D202's rule, kept on the clean path by D424; written by the D392 adapter until Step 3 is joined to production storage). |
361:A revision does not erase the earlier statement. On 2026-11-06, Alex chose to keep one evidence record for a repeat and add the new source and any missing thesis link. On 2026-11-08 the Step 2 validation showed that keeping only the earlier text discards details a later report adds, such as a guidance condition; Alex then chose to keep each repeat's own text and source inside the stored record's group, without rewriting the original ("A 继续；跑council loop 开始做"; [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §7). In the judge's ledger each member of a group is its own evidence record, related to the original as the same claim (D204), and the judge reads each copy with that note; a second or later copy of a statement already in the same request is 31% to 34% of what the judge is shown in the saved clean draws (26% to 31% in production's), and how a judgement weighs copies is not tested further (Step 2 log §17). Multiple articles reporting the same statement do not automatically establish independent original sources. The current production writer stores separate repeat records, so this is a change for the clean path and needs implementation and verification; the existing historical records remain available. Step 3 ends when the theses, evidence and relationships are saved. The grouping library (`step3/library.py`, frozen) holds them; a measurement writer (`step4-2026-11-10/stage_b/ladder_adapter.py`) writes them into the judge's ledger together with the judged questions, processing steps, completed days and closings (D429, D564, D567). Joining Step 3 to production storage is part of gate 5 (section 1.5).
